## Best-model predictions: pooled sample

This chunk reports the test-set predictions from the strongest pooled-sample model under the current results. In the pooled sample, the best overall model is the average-probability ensemble built from Random Forest, kNN, and XGBoost.


```{r}
avg_prob_yes <- function(fit_list, new_df) {
  prob_mat <- sapply(
    fit_list,
    function(fit) predict(fit, new_data = new_df, type = "prob")$.pred_yes
  )
  rowMeans(prob_mat)
}

thr_all_best <- results_all %>%
  filter(Model == "Avg Prob (RF+kNN+XGB)") %>%
  pull(Threshold)

pred_all_best <- tibble(
  truth = test_data_mod$romantic,
  prob_yes = avg_prob_yes(list(rf_fit, knn_fit, xgb_fit), test_data_mod)
) %>%
  mutate(
    pred_class = factor(
      if_else(prob_yes >= thr_all_best, "yes", "no"),
      levels = c("no", "yes")
    )
  )

cm_all_best <- yardstick::conf_mat(pred_all_best, truth = truth, estimate = pred_class)

pred_all_best %>% slice_head(n = 10)
cm_all_best
```


This table shows the actual class (`truth`), the predicted probability of being in a relationship (`prob_yes`), and the final predicted class (`pred_class`) after applying the selected threshold. The confusion matrix below summarizes how often the model correctly classified `yes` and `no` cases in the held-out pooled test set.
## Best-model predictions: female subgroup

This chunk reports the test-set predictions from the strongest final classifier in the female subgroup. In the current E6 results, Random Forest is the best female model by test accuracy.


